WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.500 --> 00:00:06.100
OAuth and OpenID Connect introduce several token-like values with different jobs.

2
00:00:06.200 --> 00:00:09.720
Access tokens authorize access to protected resources.

3
00:00:09.820 --> 00:00:13.500
ID tokens identify an authenticated user to a client.

4
00:00:13.600 --> 00:00:17.120
Refresh tokens can obtain new access tokens.

5
00:00:17.220 --> 00:00:22.660
State and nonce values help bind redirect flows and reduce request confusion.

6
00:00:22.760 --> 00:00:26.440
Treating these values as interchangeable is a design error.

7
00:00:26.720 --> 00:00:34.720
Access tokens should be scoped, audience-bound where possible, short lived, and validated by the intended resource server.

8
00:00:34.820 --> 00:00:39.780
ID tokens should not be sent to APIs as if they were access tokens.

9
00:00:39.880 --> 00:00:45.320
Refresh tokens are high-value because they can extend a session or obtain new access.

10
00:00:45.420 --> 00:00:55.020
They usually deserve stronger storage, rotation where supported, and revocation on logout, suspicious use, account changes, or compromise events.

11
00:00:55.300 --> 00:01:06.740
For modern interactive flows, Authorization Code Flow with PKCE is commonly preferred because it avoids exposing long-lived credentials to the browser and helps bind the code exchange to the client.

12
00:01:06.840 --> 00:01:14.680
OAuth state and OpenID Connect nonce values should be generated securely, stored for the flow, and validated when the redirect returns.

13
00:01:14.780 --> 00:01:20.620
These values are not decorative; they bind the response to the request the application actually initiated.

14
00:01:20.900 --> 00:01:24.740
Using an identity provider does not eliminate application responsibility.

15
00:01:24.840 --> 00:01:36.440
The Node.js app still owns local authorization, tenant boundaries, session handling, token storage, logging, account lifecycle, and incident response.

16
00:01:36.540 --> 00:01:43.820
The app must validate the right token for the right purpose and then enforce what the authenticated identity may actually do.
